{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FnumAaBR9wHHdDAzc4oWXT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me carefully analyze each RUNNING/QUEUED experiment and generate median-tier predictions based on the defined median tier characteristics:\n\n**Median tier definition:**\n- 新模型族结果混合——一个确认、一个边缘或失败\n- 辅助实验部分成功\n- universality claim 需 scope 但不崩溃\n\n**Core assumptions:**\n1. LLaMA-8B K*=2 但边缘（CI lower 0.600-0.607），或 Mistral K*=1（1v2 AUC 0.58-0.60）\n\n\n2. 两个新模型族中至少一个确认 K*=2，而 Pythia 的 decoding 实验预期 3/4 配置达到 K*=2，其中一个可能边缘\n3. Length ablation 显示 128-token 时信息不足导致 K*=1，但 256/512 token 长度应该确认 K*=2\n4. Cross-scorer 实验中的 scorer-dependence 模式预期在新实验中重现但保持可控范围\n\n**Key metric ranges:**\n- new_family_best_1v2_auc: [0.61, 0.635]\n- new_family_worst_1v2_auc: [0.58, 0.602]\n- pythia_decoding_kstar2_fraction\n\n: [0.75, 1.0]\n- length_128_1v2_auc: [0.575, 0.605]\n- total_conditions_kstar2: [12, 15]\n- cross_scorer_kstar2_fraction: [0.6, 0.85]\n\n现在我来逐个预测运行中和排队中的实验。对于 exp-018 的 LLaMA-3.1-8B，这是两个新模型族之一，根据中位数假设应该确认 K*=2 但处于边缘（CI 下界 0.600-0.607），而这个更大的 8B 模型来自不同的架构族。\n\n使用 Qwen2.5-1.5B 作为交叉评分器，我预期 0v1 会非常高（>0.96），1v2 应该在最佳新族的范围内约 0.618，2v3 会降到 0.60 以下，交叉评分器会引入轻微的信号衰减。对于 exp-019 的 Mistral-7B-v0.3，根据中位数假设这个模型确认 K*=1，1v2 AUC 在 0.58-0.60 范围内。\n\n我设定约 0.593，置信区间下限低于 0.60。Mistral 可能失败的原因包括其独特的滑动窗口注意力机制和分词器可能导致不同的信息损失模式，以及 Qwen 交叉评分器可能无法很好地捕捉 Mistral 特定的降解模式。对于 exp-020 的 Pythia-1.4B 解码消融，我在参考之前 Qwen 解码消融的 exp-003 结果，其中测试了所有 4 种配置。\n\nPythia 的整体信号较弱（核采样的 1v2 AUC 为 0.6539，而 Qwen 为 0.6957），所以我预期贪心、核采样和温度 0.7 的 K* 都会是 2，但温度 1.2 可能是边界情况——对 Qwen 来说温度 1.2 的 AUC 最高（0.7037），但对 Pythia 可能产生不同效果，所以我考虑让贪心作为边界配置。\n\n实际上，重新审视 Qwen 的解码消融模式（贪心 0.6892、核采样 0.6862、T=0.7 0.6885、T=1.2 0.7037），T=1.2 确实最高，所以我改为让 T=0.7 作为边界配置（置信区间下界刚好在 0.600 左右），这样 Pythia 的贪心配置 1v2 AUC 约为 0.641，置信区间下界约为 0.630。\n\n核采样、T=0.7 和 T=1.2 的配置都保持在 K*=2，但现在我需要检查 Pythia 在实验 001 中实际使用的是 nucleus p=0.95 而不是 0.9。\n\n当我用不同的解码策略重新校准时，贪心搜索通常会降低多样性，而更高的温度反而可能增强信号强度。对于中位数层级（3/4 K*=2，一个边界），贪心搜索和 nucleus p=0.9 的结果都确认了 K*=2 的分类。\n\n不过当我检查 T=0.7 和 T=1.2 的结果时，这样就变成了 4/4 都符合 K*=2，这与预期的 3/4 中位数不符，所以我需要调整 T=0.7 作为边界情况，让其置信区间的下界略低于 0.60，这样才能与指标范围 pythia_decoding_kstar2_fraction: [0.75, 1.0] 保持一致。\n\n现在我重新设置数据，让贪心、Nucleus p=0.9 和 T=1.2 都清晰地显示 K*=2，而 T=0.7 的置信区间下界约为 0.597，刚好低于 0.60 的阈值，作为边界情况。\n\n这反映了较低温度减少随机性，使得 Pythia 生成变得更重复可预测，可能在边界附近压缩了深度信号。\n\n对于长度消融实验，128 个 token 的置信区间在 0.576-0.600 之间，表明信息不足以达到 K*=2，而 256 和 512 个 token 都稳定地支持 K*=2。\n\n这与基线 Qwen-cont 的默认性能一致，512 个 token 应该接近默认长度，256 个略低，128 个明显更低。\n\n现在看跨评分器矩阵实验，我在检查 exp-022 中是否重现了 exp_A 的评分器依赖模式。\n\n根据之前的结果，当用 Qwen 作为评分器评估 Pythia 和 OLMo 的续写时，K* 下降到 1，而自评分或同系列评分器通常保持 K*=2。我需要预测 4×4 矩阵中哪些组合会显示 K*=1 与 K*=2 的差异。\n\n根据 cross_scorer_kstar2_fraction 在 0.6 到 0.85 之间的范围，应该有 10 到 14 个单元格显示 K*=2，其余的则是 K*=1。\n\n我在重新思考 exp-022 的实际含义——它测试的是 4 个生成器与 4 个评分器的完整矩阵，其中对角线上的自评分情况都应该是 K*=2，而同架构的交叉评分（比如 Qwen-rw 由 Qwen-cont 评分）也保持 K*=2，但跨家族的组合会产生混合结果。粗略估计大约 11 个单元格是 K*=2，即 0.6875 的比例，这落在预期范围内。\n\n现在转向 exp-008，这是一个计划中的重训练链生态有效性测试，我需要确认指令的意图——是否应该覆盖所有实验还是只关注非完成的那些。\n\n重新审视要求后，我认为应该专注于那些还有待执行的实验（RUNNING、QUEUED 和 planned 状态），因为已完成的实验已经有实际数据了。所以我会为 exp-018 到 exp-023 这些实验生成详细预测，其中 exp-008 作为计划中的辅助实验，预期会在中位数层级上\"部分成功\"，表现出相似的 K*=2 但指标更嘈杂，特征分布在提示链和重训练链之间存在差异。\n\n对于 exp-009 的污染剂量反应测试，我预期在 10% 注入时二元和渐进式过滤的差异最小，而在 50% 时二元过滤会显示明显优势，整体呈现单调的剂量反应曲线但效应量较小。exp-014 的自然验证实验使用 Common Crawl 数据，预期与 AlignScore 有中等相关性，深度预测显示合理分布但缺乏校准的真实标准。对于被遗弃的 exp-023 维基百科种子数据实验，K*=2 可能仍然成立，尽管信号强度可能有所不同。\n\n现在我在整理 YAML 输出，重新考虑 total_conditions_kstar2 指标的范围，统计已完成且满足 K*=2 条件的实验数量。\n\n从运行中和队列中的新条件来看，我需要计算额外能达到 K*=2 的实验数，这会进一步增加总数。\n\n但目标范围是 [12, 15]，所以我需要重新检查一下计数——这个指标可能是指所有实验的总数，而不仅仅是新的。让我逐个验证现有的 K*=2 条件，确保没有遗漏或重复计算。\n\n对于新实验，我需要更仔细地区分哪些是真正独立的条件，哪些只是对现有数据的重新分析或引导置信区间计算。\n\n现在看起来我在计算K*=2的条件数时可能重复计算了——如果我把跨评分器矩阵的每个单元格都算作独立条件，总数会超过目标范围[12, 15]。让我重新调整新实验中K*=2的分配，减少一些实验中的K*=2条件数，使总数落在目标范围内。 Looking at the breakdown, the original 10 conditions come from five model-mode pairs, four decoding strategies on Qwen, and one cross-scale experiment. The new experiments add nine independent conditions: one each for LLaMA and Mistral, four decoding variations for Pythia, and three length configurations. That brings the total to 19 conditions, which exceeds the target range of 12-15 for K*=2.\n\nNow I'm working through which conditions should have K*=1 versus K*=2 to hit the target. Looking at the new experiments, Mistral, Pythia at T=0.7, and the 128-token length seem like natural candidates for K*=1, which would give me 15 total K*=2 conditions. But if the 256-token condition also ends up as K*=1, I'd be at 14, which is within range. The 256-token case is borderline though—if it's K*=2 instead, I'd exceed the target. So I'm reconsidering the allocation: keeping LLaMA and Pythia greedy at K*=2 while setting Mistral and the shorter lengths to K*=1 should get me to the right count. I'm trying to find the right balance—if I make Pythia's greedy decoding also fail at K*=1, that brings me down to 14 total K*=2 conditions, which fits within the target range of 12 to 15. Let me verify this configuration: Mistral fails, Pythia at T=0.7 fails, the 128-token variant fails, the 256-token variant fails, and Pythia's greedy decoding fails, leaving me with 14 successes out of 19 total conditions. I'm tallying up the K*=2 conditions across the experiments and getting totals around 16-17, which seems higher than the reported range of 12-15. The discrepancy might be because the metric only counts newly added conditions from the current batch rather than all conditions combined, or there's something different about how the counts are being calculated.\n\nRather than getting stuck on the exact numbers, I should focus on the overall narrative of what these experiments are testing. Now I'm shifting to examine exp-022, which involves a 4×4 cross-scorer matrix with 4 different generators and 4 scorers. Since it depends on exp-021, it might leverage the length conditions from that experiment. The generators are likely a combination like Qwen-cont, Pythia, OLMo, and one more model, while the scorers would be a set of evaluation functions to test scorer independence.\n\nLooking at the results from exp_A, I can see that cross-scorer setups with continuation data show K* values dropping to 1 in some cases, while feature rankings stay quite stable. For the median tier where the cross_scorer_kstar2_fraction falls between 0.6 and 0.85, I'd expect roughly 10-14 cells out of 16 to have K*=2. The diagonal self-scorer cells should all be K*=2, same-family pairs would be K*=2, and cross-family continuation cases might dip to K*=1. That gives me around 11 out of 16, or about 0.69, which fits nicely within the target range.\n\nNow I need to write out the comprehensive YAML output covering all the planned, running, and queued experiments, starting with exp-018 through exp-019 and beyond.\n\nFor exp-018 with LLaMA-8B, I'm predicting the 0v1 comparison at around 0.972, which is high but slightly lower than what we'd see with smaller models due to the cross-scorer effect. The 1v2 comparison should land around 0.621 with a confidence interval of 0.610 to 0.632, and 2v3 around 0.558 with bounds of 0.547 to 0.569. I'm working through the remaining tier comparisons now.\n\nActually, let me recalibrate based on the assumption that the CI lower bound for LLaMA should be in the 0.600-0.607 range. That shifts my 1v2 estimate to around 0.614 with a tighter interval of 0.603 to 0.625, which puts K* at 2 since the lower bound exceeds 0.60.\n\nFor exp-019 with Mistral-7B, I'm expecting 0v1 around 0.968, but the 1v2 comparison drops to about 0.593 with a confidence interval of 0.582 to 0.604. This suggests K* would be 1 since the lower bound falls below the 0.60 threshold. Though if the worst-case 1v2 AUC range is actually between 0.58 and 0.602, that would keep 1v2 hovering around 0.593 with slightly adjusted bounds.\n\nNow looking at exp-020 with Pythia across different decoding strategies, I'm seeing K*=2 consistently across most conditions. Greedy decoding gives 1v2 around 0.638, nucleus sampling at p=0.9 pushes it to 0.651, temperature 0.7 sits at 0.613 (right at the edge with the lower bound just above 0.60), and temperature 1.2 reaches 0.668. The pattern suggests three or four conditions meet the K*=2 criterion, though the temperature 0.7 case is borderline.\n\nAdjusting the numbers slightly to make the edge case clearer: if I shift T=0.7 down to 0.608 with a lower confidence bound of 0.596, that would clearly fall below the 0.60 threshold and mark it as K*=1. This gives me exactly the 3/4 split the assumption describes—three configurations solidly at K*=2 with one marginal case. The Chinese term \"边缘\" suggests something borderline, which could mean either barely K*=2 or barely K*=1, but given the median tier context, I'm interpreting this as one configuration that's genuinely on the edge between the two categories. I'm settling on a borderline case for the Pythia decoding config where the point estimate sits above 0.60 but the confidence interval lower bound dips just below it, creating ambiguity between K*=1 and K*=2 depending on interpretation—this fits the 0.75 to 1.0 range for the kstar2_fraction. Now I'm ready to finalize the predictions and compile everything into YAML, starting with the length ablation results for 128 tokens and working through the remaining configurations. Now I'm figuring out which four models to use for the cross-scorer matrix—likely Qwen-cont, Pythia, OLMo, and either Qwen-base or Qwen-7B as generators, with a corresponding set of scorers. The key is that these models should have generated depth chains available from the earlier experiments.\n\nLooking at the patterns from exp_A, self-scoring with these models tends to stabilize around K*=2, while cross-family scoring with continuation can drop to K*=1, though feature ranking stays consistent. For a 4×4 matrix, I'm aiming for roughly 10-14 cells where K*=2 falls in that median tier between 0.6 and 0.85 fraction.\n\nSelf-scorers should all hit K*=2, similar-architecture pairs like Pythia and OLMo should too, but dissimilar cross-family combinations where the scorer architecture diverges significantly from the generator will likely drop to K*=1. That gives me around 11 out of 16 cells at K*=2, or about 0.69 fraction.\n\nFor the retrain-chain experiment, I'm expecting K*=2 to hold like it does with prompt-chain, though the signal gets noisier—the 1v2 AUC drops to around 0.63 compared to prompt-chain's 0.654, and there's a noticeable feature distribution shift with KL divergence around 0.15 between the two approaches.\n\nThe feature degradation ordering stays mostly consistent across these methods with a Spearman correlation of about 0.78, though this experiment only goes down to depth 4 rather than depth 5 like the others.\n\nLooking at the dose-response results, the binary versus graduated injection patterns show a clear monotonic trend—at 10% contamination there's barely any difference, but by 50% the binary approach pulls ahead by about 0.005 in a statistically significant way, which aligns with what I saw in exp-006.\n\nNow checking the naturalistic validation against AlignScore shows a moderate correlation...\n\nGenerating YAML structure... I'm tallying up the K*=2 conditions across the running and queued experiments—LLaMA adds one, Mistral contributes zero, and the Pythia variants with different decoding strategies mostly add one each, though the temperature 0.7 case is borderline depending on whether I'm being strict about the confidence interval. That puts me at either 16 or 17 total conditions with K*=2 depending on how I classify that edge case.\n\nBut the target range of [12, 15] suggests I'm overcounting somewhere. It's possible the original 10 conditions aren't all independent—maybe the Qwen variants are being treated as a single model with different modes rather than separate conditions. Or perhaps the new experiments only contribute 2-5 K*=2 conditions rather than the 6-7 I'm seeing, which would mean more of these runs are actually failing to replicate than I thought. I'm realizing there's a fundamental counting inconsistency here—if Pythia's greedy and lower-temperature variants both fail to meet K*=2, that contradicts my earlier assumption that 3 out of 4 Pythia conditions would satisfy it. Let me reconsider what \"total_conditions_kstar2 = 12-15\" actually refers to. Maybe it's counting both the nine new standalone experiments plus the cross-scorer matrix cells from exp-022, which would give around 25 total new cells, but the math still doesn't align cleanly when I split between cross-scorer and non-cross-scorer contributions.\n\nRather than chase down the exact arithmetic, I should focus on the narrative that matters: LLaMA's consistent confirmation, Mistral's mixed results, Pythia's decoding behavior across different sequence lengths, and how the cross-scorer matrix partially reproduces the original pattern. Now I'll move forward with writing the YAML structure and then think through what the Pythia decoding experiment should show physically.\n\nFor the Pythia decoding ablation, I'm working out the scaling: Pythia's baseline 1v2 score of 0.6539 is about 94% of Qwen's greedy performance, and I need to figure out how the different decoding strategies (greedy, nucleus sampling, temperature) would affect Pythia in continuation mode compared to what we saw in the Qwen rewrite mode experiment. For the higher temperature setting, I'm estimating around 0.665. Let me finalize these decoding strategy estimates with confidence intervals: greedy at 0.641, nucleus p=0.9 at 0.649, temperature 0.7 at 0.610 (which edges into that borderline territory), and temperature 1.2 at 0.664. That gives me three clear K*=2 cases plus one edge case where the lower bound dips below 0.60. Now I'm ready to write out the full YAML with all the experiment details and need to work through the 2v3 comparisons for each variant.\n\nLooking at the performance pattern across models, I can see the 2v3 AUC consistently runs lower than 1v2, typically falling in the 0.545-0.59 range with deltas around -0.05 to -0.10.\n\nBased on this trend, I'm projecting similar performance drops for the new experiments—LLaMA and Mistral should follow comparable patterns, while the ablations will scale proportionally with their 1v2 scores.\n\nNow I'm writing out the complete YAML configuration with all the experimental results...\n\nAdjusting the confidence intervals for LLaMA to align with the median assumptions—the 1v2 CI lower bound should sit within the 0.600-0.607 range, so I'm setting it to 0.605. For Mistral, I need to apply the same logic based on its K* value.\n\nNow I'm ready to write up the final output. For the cross-scorer matrix in exp-022, I'm treating it as a standard 4×4 setup with four generators and four scorers, assuming the dependency on exp-021 is just about shared resources rather than a fundamental change to the structure.\n\nLooking at the patterns from the existing data, I can see that self-scorer pairs consistently give K*=2, while cross-scorer combinations show more variation—particularly when pairing continuation-mode generators with different scorers, where K* often drops to 1. The AUC values tend to be higher for within-architecture pairings and lower for cross-architecture ones, especially in continuation mode.\n\nFor the 4×4 matrix with Qwen-cont, Pythia, OLMo, and a fourth model as both generators and scorers, the diagonal should all be K*=2 with AUC values around 0.61-0.65 based on the self-scorer baseline. The off-diagonal cells will depend on architecture similarity—I expect the cross-scorer pairs to show K*=2 for similar architectures but potentially K*=1 for very different ones, with corresponding AUC drops.\n\nThis distribution gives me roughly 11 cells at K*=2 and 5 at K*=1, averaging to about 0.6875, which fits nicely within the target range. Now I'm ready to construct the full YAML configuration with all these